library(tidyverse)

#' Remove YAML frontmatter from the first line of text content
#' @param skill_content A character vector of lines (first entry is the top comment or YAML frontmatter)
#' @return The vector with the YAML frontmatter removed, if present
remove_frontmatter <- function(skill_content) {
  # Identify where the YAML frontmatter ends ("---")
  yaml_end <- which(skill_content == "---")
  if (length(yaml_end) >= 2) {
    skill_content <- seq_len(nrow(skill_content)[[1]])[(yaml_end[2] + 1):nrow(skill_content)[[1]]][[1]]
  } else {
    return(skill_content[[1,]], TRUE) # Return just the first line if no frontmatter found; FALSE to indicate that there is a comment instead which you'd skip with a condition (like in your code).
}
```

---

### How it helps now:

You can call this helper anywhere you see the same pattern. For example, within an `if` block or `else`:

```r
content <- remove_frontmatter(skill_content)
# ...
```

No need to repeat regex logic on every file/line where text is parsed. Also, consider adding a small guard:

```r
remove_frontmatter <- function(skill_content) {
  # Identify the closing "---" that ends the frontmatter block
  yaml_end <- which(attr(skill_content, "type") %in% "yaml")
  if (length(yaml_end) > 0) {
    skill_content <- skills[1:(yaml_end[[1]] - 1)]
    return(list(content = skill_content, has_frontmatter = TRUE))
  } else {
    return(list(content = NA_character_, has_frontmatter = FALSE))
  }
}
```

This returns both the cleaned content and whether frontmatter existed, so you can always decide what to do next without checking `length(skill_content)`.

